# 19. Design principles behind this organization

This guide follows common workflow-engineering ideas used by mature bioinformatics workflow ecosystems:

1. **Separate workflow logic, configuration, software environments, scripts and documentation.**
2. **Keep paths predictable and hierarchical.**
3. **Version-control reproducibility files, not large raw/intermediate data.**
4. **Use versioned containers/environments for portability.**
5. **Keep active workflow paths stable; organize navigation around them before physically moving files.**
6. **Prefer modular workflow components when the project grows.**

Useful official references:

- Snakemake — Best practices  
  https://snakemake.readthedocs.io/en/stable/snakefiles/best_practices.html

- Snakemake — Modularization  
  https://snakemake.readthedocs.io/en/stable/snakefiles/modularization.html

- Snakemake — Configuration  
  https://snakemake.readthedocs.io/en/stable/snakefiles/configuration.html

- nf-core — Pipeline file structure  
  https://nf-co.re/docs/developing/pipelines/template-files

The goal is not to copy another workflow framework literally. The useful principle is the same: **predictable structure + separation of concerns + reproducibility + documentation**.

